-\section{Computing resources and the scope of the findings} \label{sec:ch4-boundaries}
-The project used HFU server resources. The later ViFailback study recorded a single NVIDIA Tesla T4 with 16~GiB of device memory. Reusing frozen camera features and compact sensor statistics made repeated fitting practical. Dataset acquisition and preparation also required resources: MSM used a bounded download plan for selected laboratory files, while REASSEMBLE later released approximately 66.9~GiB of derived caches, retaining source videos, checkpoints, predictions, and recovery metadata. Rebuilding those caches adds work to a fresh run.
-ViFailback logged about 303.8 elapsed hours across two GPU controller processes, including decoding, validation, and checkpointing; this was neither GPU kernel time nor total thesis compute. Completing its remaining \texttt{FULL20} matrix was estimated to require another 43 GPU days on the single T4, with 47 days allowed for variation and overhead. This forecast of additional work informed the decision to stop further CNN fitting. It is a resource estimate, not a model-performance result.
-The implementation contribution includes acquiring usable observations, maintaining aligned sample lists, reusing features, and preserving evidence within these limits. Scientific findings still depend on the completed comparisons. Limited resources do not establish why a model performed poorly, and an unassessed configuration has no measured result. Head-only timings exclude frame reading and feature extraction; operational timing would also need acquisition and preprocessing costs. Chapters~6 and~8 report study-specific training coverage and timing boundaries.
+\section{Computing limits and the scope of the findings} \label{sec:ch4-boundaries}
+Keeping the camera backbone fixed allowed the study to extract its features once and reuse them while fitting prediction heads across recording groups. The statistical sensor model needed only 220 values per action. Both choices made repeated comparisons practical. They also define the conditions under which the results hold. A temporal model trained for a limited number of epochs may behave differently with more training. A configuration that never reached assessment has no measured result.
+Computing time needs the same care as prediction accuracy. Timing a head on saved features excludes the work of reading the frames and extracting those features. Timing the whole model after a completed action still excludes the time spent waiting for the action to finish. A claim about an operational warning system would need to include acquisition, synchronisation, preprocessing, and inference up to the moment a decision becomes available.
+The clearest findings in this thesis come from comparisons in which the competing models saw the same assessment cases and the difference between them can be stated precisely. The 753-action REASSEMBLE follow-up, for example, can show how a change affected those previously studied actions. A fresh test of the resulting design would require the target, decision time, recording groups, models, fault conditions, and main performance measure to be fixed before opening new assessment data. The case-study chapters use these boundaries to explain what each experiment established and which questions still need new evidence.
